pide_bridge — implementation plan
=================================

goal
----

Make the Scala-to-Isabelle/ML boundary one independently testable component.
Every call must be correlated, bounded, cancellable, deadline-bound, and failed
when the bridge stops.  Application services use typed operations and never
own protocol promises, raw command names, or protocol-output handlers.

starting point
--------------

The connection-kernel remediation already replaced FIFO promise lists with
request-unique UUID routes, propagated connection cancellation through every
current prover-backed path, cancelled all route tables during backend stop, and
added live reverse/concurrent correlation and shutdown tests.  This plan keeps
those guarantees green while removing their remaining duplication.

The residual architecture is still incomplete:

- `MCP_Session` owns seven `BridgeRoutes[A]` values and one protocol handler;
- each operation defines its own positional `Bytes` request and reply shape;
- the wire has IDs but no independent bridge revision or common validation;
- direct callers can wait without a bridge-local deadline or admission bound;
- reply bodies are decoded without an explicit encoded-size limit; and
- transport delivery, pending-call ownership, and domain services compile in
  the same class.

architecture record
-------------------

The dependency direction is:

    application/domain services
              |
              v
      BridgeOperation[A]
              |
              v
          PideBridge              control plane
              |
              v
        PideTransport             data plane
              |
              v
    Isabelle protocol commands and outputs

`BridgeOperation[A]` owns the stable internal operation name plus typed payload
and result codecs.  `PideBridge` owns all correctness state and exposes
`Either[BridgeFailure, A]` to its callers.  `PideTransport` can send encoded
bytes, install one inbound receiver, and report transport termination, but
cannot inspect call ownership or domain values.  The production adapter uses
`Session.protocol_command_raw` and `Session.Protocol_Handler`; scripted tests
substitute an in-memory transport without an Isabelle process.  Transport
close detaches bridge delivery; it does not own `Headless.Session.stop`, which
remains a composition-root responsibility because the current backend also
uses that session for direct PIDE operations.

state and ordering
------------------

Bridge state is `Starting`, `Open`, `Stopping`, or `Stopped`.

- `Starting` owns exactly one bounded hello exchange and admits no ordinary
  call.  A concurrent readiness waiter cannot replace that hello owner.
- `Open` admits only while `pending < maxPending`.
- Admission allocates an unguessable internal call ID, validates and encodes
  the request, checks its serialized size, registers one typed pending
  completion, arms one deadline, and only then sends the envelope.  Initial
  send is linearized before cancellation for the same ID: cancellation that
  already won emits no call; cancellation after enqueue emits one cancel.
- Results may arrive in any order.  Only the matching ID and operation can
  transition the pending call; there is no global response-order guarantee.
- `Stopping` admits nothing.  It atomically removes and fails every pending
  caller, cancels deadlines, asks ML to interrupt every admitted worker, and
  requests a drain acknowledgement after those ordered cancels.
- The ML drain acknowledgement is published only when its call-route and
  worker registry is empty.  `Stopped` is reached after that acknowledgement,
  or after the composition root terminates the Isabelle session and the
  transport reports termination.  A drain timeout reports failure and remains
  `Stopping`; it cannot assert `INV3` merely because Scala promises are gone.
- The composition root reports the session-termination fallback only after
  `Headless.Session.stop` returns successfully.  If session termination raises,
  the bridge remains `Stopping` so an unproven termination cannot satisfy
  `INV3`; a later stop attempt may retry the termination step.
- Repeated stop is idempotent and observes the same terminal stop outcome.

wire and compatibility
----------------------

Use one internal v1 YXML envelope carried as the sole byte argument of
`MCP.bridge`.  Its kind is `hello`, `call`, `cancel`, or `drain`; every kind
contains the bridge revision and the identifiers needed for correlation.
Calls also contain operation and typed payload.  One `MCP.bridge_result`
function carries a sole YXML envelope containing revision, kind, call or
control ID, operation where applicable, status, and typed payload.

The bridge revision is not the MCP revision.  A public MCP revision can reuse a
bridge revision, and a bridge representation can evolve without changing the
client protocol.  Wrong revisions and unknown operations return correlated
protocol failures whenever the call ID can be recovered.  Malformed envelopes
without a usable ID are reported as internal protocol diagnostics and cannot
complete another call.

`MCP_Tools` defines `MCP_Bridge`, the command, result encoder, executor, and an
extensible registry from stable operation name to a payload handler.  It
registers the six base operations.  A descendant theory may add an operation;
duplicate names fail at theory build.  `MCP_Repl` registers IR, but contributes
only decode, dispatch, and output-capture behavior.  The common executor owns
the sole Future group and cancellation route, so wrapping IR cannot create a
nested route or double-register the ID.

The configured theory is the immutable registry-root theory defined by
`plans/context_locator`, not the connection's agent context.  Operation
payloads carry opaque context locators where domain behavior needs a
Proof.context.  They never carry designation variants or bundle lists; ML owns
context-locator resolution before invoking the registered domain handler.

After the configured target theory is available, Scala sends a hello with a
bounded startup deadline.  The reply contains the bridge revision and sorted
registered operation names.  The composition root supplies the required
operation set for its selected backend (`MCP-Tools` excludes IR; `MCP-HOL`
requires it).  `PideBridge` is not Ready until revision and required operations
match.  A stale heap with no `MCP.bridge` command therefore fails backend
readiness at the hello deadline instead of deferring the problem to a public
request.  This internal compatibility check is not a public service catalog.

failure and cancellation matrix
-------------------------------

`PideBridge.call[A]` returns `BridgeResult[A]`, defined as
`Either[BridgeFailure, A]`.  Terminal bridge failures are typed values:
`Cancelled`, `TimedOut`, `SessionStopped`, `Overloaded`, `TooLarge`,
`TransportFailed`, `RemoteError`, and `ProtocolError`.  Domain adapters are the
only code that may translate them to public MCP results and may not infer a
failure kind from display text.  An operation-domain error is part of a
successfully decoded `A` (for example `Right(MCP_Session.Error(message))`), not
a `RemoteError`.  `RemoteError` means the ML bridge executor itself caught a
non-interrupt operation exception before it could produce `A`.

The outer result status algebra is `ok`, `remote_error`, `protocol_error`, or
`too_large`.  Only `ok` invokes the operation result decoder; the others carry
a small bridge failure payload.  A malformed, wrong-operation, wrong-revision,
or invalid-status reply with a known ID removes and fails that matching call
with `ProtocolError` and requests cancellation of its ML worker.  Unknown,
duplicate, and late IDs have no matching owner, so they emit invariant
diagnostics and otherwise do nothing.

The pending registry is the terminal race owner.  Normal reply, client
cancellation, safety deadline, send failure, later transport termination, and
stop all compete to remove the same entry; only the winner completes it.
Losing late replies emit diagnostic invariant evidence and are discarded.
Cancellation never reuses a client JSON-RPC ID on the internal wire.  The
tests make the already-cancelled, cancel/result, timeout/result, stop/result,
send-failure, post-stop, and repeated-stop races explicit.

policy and resource bounds
--------------------------

`PideBridgePolicy` is immutable and validated before bridge construction.  It
contains `maxPending`, `callTimeout`, `drainTimeout`, `maxRequestBytes`, and
`maxReplyBytes`.  There is no waiting queue: overload is immediate.  The
composition root derives bridge admission and deadline defaults from the
already validated server options, while keeping the policy type independent of
`ConnectionPolicy`.  `drainTimeout` is the non-negative
`mcp_shutdown_drain` duration: an explicitly configured zero still permits a
synchronous acknowledgement, otherwise it selects immediate forced session
termination; the repository default remains 10 seconds.

The size of an envelope is exactly the raw byte length of the sole canonical
YXML value after serialization and before `protocol_command_raw` send or YXML
decode; the fixed command/function name is outside the configured limit.
Scala checks requests before send and ML checks them again before decode.  ML
checks encoded successful replies before `Output.protocol_message` and emits a
small correlated `too_large` envelope instead; Scala checks all received bytes
again before envelope or domain decode.  These limits bound bridge envelopes,
not memory allocated inside an operation before it produces its result, nor
the fixed buffering required by Isabelle to deliver one protocol message.

The envelope-size question remains open until representative tool catalogs,
source/document reads, and large prover results are measured.  Add a reusable
`tools/pide-bridge measure` launcher that records the serialized request and
reply byte counts for all seven operations plus committed large tool-catalog,
source, documentation, and prover-output fixtures in
`plans/evidence/pide_bridge_payloads.json`.  Resolve `Q1` by setting each
default to the next power of two at or above twice the corresponding recorded
maximum, recording the corpus revision and chosen values in that artifact.
Implementation must not silently pick a convenient test-only value.

implementation checkpoints
--------------------------

1. Add characterization fixtures for every current operation and list-change
   event.  Extract `PideTransport`, `PideBridgePolicy`, failure types, and a
   generic pending registry while preserving the current wire.
2. Move Scala operation codecs behind `BridgeOperation[A]`; migrate every
   route to one bridge control plane and delete the seven maps from
   `MCP_Session`.
3. Define the extensible ML operation registry and common executor in
   `MCP_Tools`; register base operations there, register IR from `MCP_Repl`
   without nested call ownership, and migrate to the single versioned
   request/result envelope.
4. Add the bounded hello/readiness check, bridge-local admission, safety
   deadlines, structured failures, drain acknowledgement, forced session-stop
   fallback, and idempotent stop.
5. Implement request/reply size validation on both sides.  Add the measurement
   launcher and committed corpus artifact, resolve the envelope-size question,
   and fold the derived defaults into policy and boundary tests.
6. Run focused Scala, ML, and live bridge tests after each checkpoint.  Finish
   with an adversarial review, all static gates, and the complete
   `tools/planning-gate done` command.

non-goals
---------

- Changing the public MCP protocol revision or JSON-RPC lifecycle.
- Defining immutable public tool/resource catalogs; that is the later
  `service_catalog` work package.
- Narrowing theory, scope, or REPL mutation locks; that is operation isolation.
- Exposing the internal bridge envelope to MCP clients.
